g that runs like a power source that keeps your body going. And that's your old Ngura Kul history, your language history, your sermon history, your skin name ceremony, and your law, your jurisdiction. We'll be right back."}, {"start": "00:27:37", "end": "00:29:49", "text": "To exist in the homeland of the kangaroo, you have to understand. To understand it, we'll try and teach you to soar really high like the wet stem. It's like reading a book, all these texts. The behavior of animals, the behavior of... Once you master that, you adapt to this country really well. law from this country. It shapes us who we are. They call this crown land. I sometimes wonder what what crown? I ask you to find out the original ground of this country. In doing that, you are like a milbury, fulfilling your duty of care, we'll call it. The grand result is you've fulfilled something, your fulfillment of caring your country, giving back to your country. And that is all the knowledge for your own well-being from this country, to give it back to people. In doing so, you already got the original crown, the emus crown."}, {"start": "00:30:01", "end": "00:31:40", "text": "The original crown is a southern cross here, because it's sitting on the emu's head. Yeah, to us, that's a whole murakulu. It's a reminder that we mustn't be like a little star tripping a wave. that's why the little stars is the homeland or this continent and to learn all that one will expand your learning how you should live in this country and firstly you must learn all these archives in the whole the law the skin name the land the ceremony and the language We'll be right back. the omner and the kangaroo, you have to understand the immune."}, {"start": "00:31:52", "end": "00:32:44", "text": "Your country will know you. Himmelga nijara dela, himmelga nijara dela. Himmelga nijara dela, himmelga nijara dela."}, {"start": "04:26:20", "end": "04:26:40", "text": "Mmmmmmmmmmmmmmmm"}, {"start": "04:45:09", "end": "04:45:47", "text": "